fn generate_github_alternative_url(url: &str) -> Option<(ureq::Agent, String)> {
  if !url.starts_with("https://github.com/") {
    return None;
  }

  generate_github_mirror_url_from_template(url)
    .or_else(|| generate_github_mirror_url_from_base(url))
    .map(|_alt_url| {
      (
        ureq::Agent::config_builder()
          .user_agent(BUNDLER_USER_AGENT)
          .build()
          .into(),
        url.to_owned(),
      )
    })
}

findings.body:
`generate_github_alternative_url` still computes the mirror URL. The closure then renames it to `_alt_url` and returns `url.to_owned()`, the original github.com URL:

::diff{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/utils/http_utils.rs" lines="45-60"}

The result: with `TAURI_BUNDLER_TOOLS_GITHUB_MIRROR` or `TAURI_BUNDLER_TOOLS_GITHUB_MIRROR_TEMPLATE` set (both documented in `crates/tauri-cli/ENVIRONMENT_VARIABLES.md`), `download()` logs and requests `https://github.com/...` anyway. People who use these variables because they can't reach GitHub (the original motivation was mainland China) will find WiX and NSIS downloads time out or fail. Nothing tells them the mirror was ignored.

The mirror branch also builds its agent without `.proxy(...)` or platform certs, so this path now hits GitHub directly and skips the proxy settings that `base_ureq_agent` would apply.

::callout{type="warning"}
This is a behavioral code change hidden inside a release PR whose description lists only version bumps. None of the changelog entries mention it. The current tests only cover `generate_github_mirror_url_from_template`, so they wouldn't catch it.

::suggestion{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/utils/http_utils.rs" lines="52-59"}
    .map(|alt_url| {
      (
        ureq::Agent::config_builder()
          .user_agent(BUNDLER_USER_AGENT)
          .build()
          .into(),
        alt_url,
      )
    })

The `tauri` crate isn't part of this release: `crates/tauri/Cargo.toml` stays at `2.9.2` and no change file targets it. Even so, `metadata-v2.json` now says `"tauri": "2.9.3"`:

::diff{file="crates/tauri-cli/metadata-v2.json" lines="1-9"}

The CLI compiles this file in with `include_str!`. `tauri init` (`crates/tauri-cli/src/init.rs:225`) and `tauri plugin init` (`crates/tauri-cli/src/plugin/init.rs:124`) use `metadata.tauri` to write `tauri = { version = "2.9.3" }` into new projects. Until a tauri 2.9.3 is published, every project created with CLI 2.9.3 fails to build, because Cargo can't resolve that version.

covector wouldn't have produced this change. In `.changes/config.json`, `sync-cli-metadata.js` updates the `tauri` key only from the `tauri` package's own `postversion` hook. In this release the hook runs only for `@tauri-apps/cli`, so only `cli.js.version` should change.

::suggestion{file="crates/tauri-cli/metadata-v2.json" lines="6-6"}
  "tauri": "2.9.2",

The `#[cfg(target_os = "windows")]` gates on `HashAlgorithm::Sha256` and its match arm were removed:

::diff{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/utils/http_utils.rs" lines="97-129"}

It still compiles: `sha2` is an unconditional dependency and the enum has `#[allow(dead_code)]`. But the only caller is the Windows-only `bundle/windows/msi/mod.rs:263`, so on every other platform the variant is now dead code that the allow attribute hides. The bigger problem is that this is an undocumented code change in a version-bump PR. Either revert it or move it to its own PR with a reason and a change file.
